<h1>Filling five times the capacity, profitably</h1>
<p>A route-to-market profitability and capacity planning proposal for Raincheck Earth. It connects shipments, freight, state costs and distributor margins to contribution per case, so growth is chosen by profit and not by volume alone.</p>
<p class="meta">Prepared by Malhar (Bibhabasu Bhattacharya) · Business Analyst application · Based on public sources; all model figures are illustrative until validated with company data</p>
</div>
</header>

<section id="brief"><div class="wrap">
<h2>Executive summary</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><b class="n">~91%</b>Reported sales against reported capacity (about 20,000 of 22,000 cases)<br><small>Brews &amp; Spirits, 2026; Outlook Business, Oct 2025</small></div>
<div class="card"><b class="n">5×</b>Planned capacity increase, to roughly 110,000 cases<br><small>Brews &amp; Spirits, 2026 (scenario arithmetic)</small></div>
<div class="card"><b class="n">13</b>Duty-free markets across India and the Middle East<br><small>Brews &amp; Spirits, 2026</small></div>
<div class="card"><b class="n">5%</b>Forecast yearly volume growth for premium-and-above gin in India, 2023–28<br><small>IWSR</small></div>
</div>
<p style="margin-top:24px">Raincheck has a distinctive product: gin made with harvested rainwater and Northeast Indian botanicals, sold in a reusable steel bottle, now with a Sohiong berry expression. The central planning question is which markets and channels can absorb a fivefold capacity expansion while still making money after freight, state fees and distributor costs. I propose a decision dashboard that answers it market by market.</p>
</div></section>

<section id="capacity"><div class="wrap">
<h2>The capacity question</h2>
<p>If the reported case figures share a basis, Raincheck is already close to its current ceiling. A fivefold expansion would leave roughly 90,000 additional cases to sell above today’s reported level.</p>
<div class="bars" id="capBars" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart comparing reported sales, current capacity and planned capacity in cases"></div>
<div class="note">This is a scenario calculation, not a sales forecast. Public figures are rounded and unaudited.</div>
</div></section>

<section id="market"><div class="wrap">
<h2>Market signals</h2>
<p>The category is growing, but crowded shelves mean growth does not guarantee profitable sell-through in every new outlet or state.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<tr><th>Signal</th><th>What public sources report</th><th>Implication</th><th>Source</th></tr>
<tr><td>Category growth</td><td>Premium-and-above gin volumes grew 8% in 2023; 5% yearly growth forecast for 2023–28</td><td>Tailwind, but moderating</td><td>IWSR</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shelf pressure</td><td>Crowded gin shelves have led to listing cuts in some markets</td><td>New listings can be lost</td><td>IWSR</td></tr>
<tr><td>Competition</td><td>31 Indian-made gins in one current guide</td><td>Differentiation must pay for itself</td><td>Condé Nast Traveller</td></tr>
<tr><td>Price point</td><td>Cherrapunji from ₹3,100 per 750 ml in Meghalaya; prices vary by state</td><td>Net realization differs by state</td><td>Condé Nast Traveller</td></tr>
<tr><td>Logistics</td><td>High freight from Meghalaya, difficult routes through dry states, state-by-state registration and transport rules</td><td>Cost to serve varies sharply</td><td>Outlook Business</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reach</td><td>Locator lists Meghalaya, Goa, Delhi, Mumbai, Pune, Gurgaon; duty-free in 13 markets</td><td>Broad footprint, unknown depth</td><td>Store locator; Brews &amp; Spirits</td></tr>
</table></div>
</div></section>

<section id="gap"><div class="wrap">
<h2>The research gap</h2>
<p>Public sources do not show sell-through, repeat orders, net revenue per case, cost to serve, or profit by state, channel and product. That does not mean Raincheck lacks this data internally. It gives a testable hypothesis:</p>
<div class="formula">Growth decisions should connect demand and distribution to contribution margin, not to shipments or new listings alone.</div>
</div></section>

<section id="solution"><div class="wrap">
<h2>The solution: a Route-to-Market Profitability and Capacity Planning dashboard</h2>
<p>For each market × channel × product × account, the dashboard combines these inputs:</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Demand</h3>Cases shipped, retailer or distributor sell-through, outlet availability, reorder timing, inventory</div>
<div class="card"><h3>Revenue</h3>Company net realization after applicable deductions</div>
<div class="card"><h3>Cost</h3>Product, packaging and production cost; freight and handling per case</div>
<div class="card"><h3>Market fixed costs</h3>Distributor margin, registration, annual activation</div>
</div>
<h3 style="margin-top:28px">Key measures</h3>
<div class="formula">Contribution per case = net realization − product and packaging cost − freight − variable selling cost</div>
<div class="formula">Annual contribution = cases sold × contribution per case − annual market-entry and activation cost</div>
<div class="formula">Break-even cases = annual fixed market cost ÷ contribution per case</div>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<tr><th>Metric</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Contribution per case</td><td>Does each case earn money after cost to serve?</td><td>Ranking markets and channels</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reorder rate, time to reorder</td><td>Is demand repeating or is stock sitting?</td><td>Account replenishment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cases per active account, stockout rate</td><td>Is sales effort producing depth?</td><td>Sales focus</td></tr>
<tr><td>Freight per case, inventory days</td><td>Where is cost or cash trapped?</td><td>Logistics planning</td></tr>
<tr><td>Capacity readiness</td><td>Does forecast demand justify added capacity?</td><td>Timing of expansion</td></tr>
</table></div>
<div class="note"><b>Decision rule:</b> expand or invest in a market only when its expected contribution covers its registration, logistics and account-support costs, and the sales evidence supports the forecast.</div>
</div></section>

